  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nFreeze warnings have been issued across the entire area overnight \ninto early Tuesday morning. Clear skies, dry air, low dew points, \nand light winds overnight will allow for efficient radiational \ncooling with temperatures dropping into the lower 30s for many \nplaces. The typical cold spots and the higher elevations of the West \nVirginia mountains will drop into the 20s. Take steps to protect \nsensitive vegetation from the cold.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nOver the next two days, the combination of low afternoon RH and \nbreezy winds may create elevated fire weather concerns.\n\nToday and Tuesday, afternoon relative humidity values will bottom \nout in the upper 20s and lower 30s across the lowlands and the \nmiddle 30s in the mountains. Winds will be breezy over the next \nseveral days, occasionally gusting 20-25 mph. \n\nAn approaching disturbance will increase RH by Wednesday with rain \nshowers approaching the area. Fire weather concerns will dampen by \nWednesday for most of West Virginia, except for extreme southern \nWest Virginia and southwest Virginia, where the rain may not reach. \nWidespread low relative humidity and elevated fire weather concerns \nmay return again Thursday and Friday as dry weather returns.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nAn approaching 500-mb shortwave will bring chances of showers and \nperhaps a few thunderstorms Wednesday. A lack of instability should \nprevent severe convective development. Another chance of rain will \narrive Saturday and potentially lingering through Sunday with an \napproaching cold front.\n\nThis rain is certainly much needed. Most of West Virginia is \ncurrently categorized in either D0 Abnormally Dry or D1 Moderate \nDrought according to the latest drought monitor.",
